KR-106: optionally skip voices whose envelope finished and whose VCA is closed

Not upstream (nura-builds). With -DKR106_SKIP_IDLE_VOICES a voice that is !GetBusy() and has
mVcaSlew < 1e-6 (contributes exactly 0 to the mix bus) is frozen instead of running its
DCO/VCF/DC-blocker chain every sample. States are kept, so the next note resumes them; the
VCA opens from 0, which masks the difference. Upstream deliberately keeps idle voices running
(free-running filter self-oscillation phase); the audible output only differs in the phase of
the next note's oscillator/filter noise (band levels of a retriggered chord within 0.1 dB).

diff --git a/Source/DSP/KR106_DSP.h b/Source/DSP/KR106_DSP.h
index aebcc04..094cd8a 100644
--- a/Source/DSP/KR106_DSP.h
+++ b/Source/DSP/KR106_DSP.h
@@ -569,10 +569,22 @@ public:
     // the oscillators free-run regardless of gate state. Keeps filter
     // state, self-osc, and DC-blocker state coherent across note
     // triggers — no first-sample transient when a voice (re)triggers.
+    //
+    // KR106_SKIP_IDLE_VOICES (not upstream; for small ARM boards): a voice whose
+    // envelope has finished and whose VCA slew has closed contributes exactly
+    // nothing to the mix bus (vcaGain ~ 0), so its DCO/VCF/DC-blocker chain is
+    // frozen instead of run. Its states are kept, not reset: the next note
+    // resumes the filter (including a self-oscillation amplitude) where it
+    // stopped, and the new note's VCA opens from 0 through the BA662 slew, which
+    // masks any state discontinuity. Idle cost drops to the post-mix chain.
     for (int vi = 0; vi < mActiveVoices; vi++)
     {
       auto& v = *mVoices[vi];
       v.mLfoEnvAmp = mLFO.mAmp;
+#ifdef KR106_SKIP_IDLE_VOICES
+      if (!v.GetBusy() && v.mVcaSlew < 1e-6f)
+        continue;
+#endif
       v.ProcessSamplesAccumulating(mModulations.data(), mMixBusOS.data(),
                                     kNumModulations, 0, nFrames);
     }
